/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  THE VERTICAL SPIKE for general salt reconstruction (2026-07-27, ADR
  docs/design/general-salt-reconstruction-proposal.md).

  Every reactive case before this one had a component-to-species map that was
  a RENAMING: one component, one master, coefficient 1.

      NH3  -> NH4+          acetic -> Acetate-
      CO2  -> HCO3-         H2S    -> HS-

  CaCO3 is the first that is not.  Dissolved calcium carbonate IS calcium and
  carbonate at once, so its column spans TWO families:

              CO2   CaCO3
      HCO3  [  1      1  ]         m = A n
      Ca    [  0      1  ]

  Two things the student should read off that matrix:

  (1) BOTH components feed the carbonate total.  Feeding CaCO3 raises the
      carbonate family exactly as feeding CO2 does -- which is why the engine
      ACCUMULATES into a total instead of assigning it.  A renaming cannot
      express that.

  (2) A is FULL COLUMN RANK, so reading the converged species state back as
      (CO2, CaCO3) is UNIQUE and no convention is needed.  That is a property
      of THIS system, not a general truth: with two cations and two anions
      (K/Na x Cl/SO4) the rank drops by exactly (c-1)(a-1) = 1 and the labels
      become a choice.  The engine computes the rank and refuses when it is
      deficient, rather than assuming.

  The H+ leg of calcite dissolution (CaCO3 + H+ = Ca+2 + HCO3-) is NOT in the
  map: H+ is the shared mediator of every family and is closed by
  electroneutrality, never by a family total.  That is why the pH moves when
  the calcite dissolves, without anyone writing an equation for it.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

recordType    thermophysicalPropertySystem;
schemaVersion 2;

components    ( CO2  CaCO3  water );

equilibrium
{
    formulation electrolyteGammaPhi;

    aqueous
    {
        activityModel { model davies; }
    }

    vapour    { fugacityModel idealGas; }

    volatiles ( CO2  water );
}
